{"attack": true, "case": "The target's analogy is internally incoherent in its own terms. The mechanism states that \"the 'fittest' (most tested) theories are the only ones available for further mutation and testing.\" This parenthetical equates evolutionary fitness with being 'most tested,' but in natural selection fitness is measured by reproductive success—an outcome—not by the quantity of selection pressures encountered. An organism exposed to many predators and killed is 'most tested' but not fit; likewise a theory subjected to many tests and frequently refuted is 'most tested' but would not survive any selection process. The target conflates exposure to selection pressure (testing) with surviving selection pressure (corroboration), collapsing the very distinction its analogy requires. Moreover, the empirical claim that only the most-tested theories are 'available' for further work is false: falsified, abandoned, and poorly tested theories all remain available for revision and retesting, and scientists routinely return to them. This is distinct from the prior objection's normative/inductive concern: it identifies a category error and a factual error internal to the analogy's own mechanics.", "counterexample": ["Phlogiston theory was among the most extensively tested frameworks in 18th-century chemistry, yet it was refuted and replaced—not because it was 'most tested' but because it failed the tests. It remained available for modification (and indeed was modified repeatedly) despite failing corroboration, contradicting the claim that only the most-tested theories survive or are available for further work."], "premise": "The target equates 'fittest' with 'most tested' and claims only most-tested theories are available for further work, but in evolutionary biology fitness denotes survival/reproductive success (an outcome), not exposure to challenges; and all theories remain available for revision regardless of testing history.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "7eb0b97242ae13e20d24010142b16bbc93aef8d9e65f98325d8bec5a66194ba3", "quote": "the 'fittest' (most tested) theories are the only ones available for further mutation and testing"}], "successor_question": "If fitness in the analogy properly means corroboration (surviving tests) rather than being 'most tested' (exposure to tests), can the analogy still support the claim that scientific preference is a causal survival trait rather than a normative judgment, given that the selection criterion itself (why corroboration rather than convenience or authority) would then require independent justification?"}